## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. There exists a $p \times n$ matrix $A$ such that $g(x) = f(x) + Ax$ is an immersion on all of $\mathbb{R}^n$. Moreover, the set of such matrices $A$ is residual (a countable intersection of open dense sets) in $\mathbb{R}^{p \times n} \cong \mathbb{R}^{pn}$, so no smallness constraint on the entries $a_{ij}$ is needed.

---

### Setup

The map $g(x) = f(x) + Ax$ is an immersion iff the derivative
$$dg_x = Df_x + A : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^p$$
is injective for every $x \in \mathbb{R}^n$, i.e., the $p \times n$ matrix $Df_x + A$ has full column rank $n$ for every $x$.

Let $\Sigma \subset \mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$ denote the set of $p \times n$ matrices of rank $< n$ (equivalently, rank $\le n-1$). We need $A$ such that
$$Df_x + A \notin \Sigma \quad \text{for all } x \in \mathbb{R}^n.$$

### Codimension of $\Sigma$

The set of $p \times n$ matrices of rank exactly $r$ is a smooth submanifold of $\mathbb{R}^{pn}$ of dimension $r(p+n-r)$. Hence $\Sigma$, the set of matrices of rank $\le n-1$, is a stratified set whose top stratum (rank $= n-1$) has dimension
$$(n-1)(p + n - (n-1)) = (n-1)(p+1).$$
The codimension of this top stratum in $\mathbb{R}^{pn}$ is
$$pn - (n-1)(p+1) = p - (n-1) = p - n + 1.$$
Since $2n \le p$, we have
$$\operatorname{codim}(\Sigma) = p - n + 1 \ge 2n - n + 1 = n + 1 > n.$$

### Transversality argument

Define the map
$$\Phi : \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^{p \times n} \to \mathbb{R}^{p \times n}, \qquad \Phi(x, A) = Df_x + A.$$
This map is a **submersion**: for fixed $(x, A)$, the partial derivative with respect to $A$ is the identity on $\mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$, so $D\Phi_{(x,A)}$ is surjective. A submersion is transversal to every submanifold of the target; in particular $\Phi$ is transversal to $\Sigma$.

By the **parametric transversality theorem**, for almost every $A \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$ (i.e., outside a set of measure zero), the sliced map
$$\Phi_A : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^{p \times n}, \qquad \Phi_A(x) = Df_x + A$$
is transversal to $\Sigma$.

When $\Phi_A$ is transversal to $\Sigma$, the preimage $\Phi_A^{-1}(\Sigma)$ is a submanifold of $\mathbb{R}^n$ of codimension $\operatorname{codim}(\Sigma) = p - n + 1$. But
$$\operatorname{codim}(\Sigma) = p - n + 1 > n,$$
so a submanifold of $\mathbb{R}^n$ with codimension strictly greater than $n$ must be **empty**. Therefore
$$\Phi_A^{-1}(\Sigma) = \varnothing,$$
which means $Df_x + A \notin \Sigma$ for all $x$, i.e., $Df_x + A$ has full rank $n$ for all $x$. Hence $g$ is an immersion.

### Handling non-compactness of $\mathbb{R}^n$

The parametric transversality theorem in its basic form applies to compact source manifolds. To handle $\mathbb{R}^n$, exhaust it by compact sets: let $K_i = \overline{B(0,i)}$, $i = 1, 2, \ldots$. For each $K_i$, define
$$U_i = \{A \in \mathbb{R}^{p \times n} : Df_x + A \text{ has rank } n \text{ for all } x \in K_i\}.$$

- **$U_i$ is open:** Full rank is an open condition, and it holds pointwise on the compact set $K_i$, so the set of $A$ achieving it is open.
- **$U_i$ is dense:** Restrict $\Phi$ to $K_i \times \mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$. The same submersion argument and parametric transversality (now on the compact set $K_i$) show that the complement of $U_i$ has measure zero, hence $U_i$ is dense.

By the **Baire category theorem**, $\bigcap_{i=1}^{\infty} U_i$ is dense (in particular, nonempty) in $\mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$. Any $A$ in this intersection makes $Df_x + A$ full rank for all $x \in K_i$ for every $i$, hence for all $x \in \mathbb{R}^n$.

### Why no $\epsilon$-bound is needed

The argument shows the set of valid $A$ is **residual** (dense $G_\delta$) in the **entire** space $\mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$, not merely in a small ball $\{|a_{ij}| < \epsilon\}$. The smallness constraint $|a_{ij}| < \epsilon$ is sometimes imposed when one wants to perturb $f$ only slightly (to preserve proximity to the original map, as in Whitney's weak immersion theorem). But for the **existence** question alone, no such restriction is required: the codimension condition $p - n + 1 > n$ (guaranteed by $2n \le p$) forces the "bad" set to be avoidable by a generic — and in particular, possibly large — perturbation $A$.

### Conclusion

Such a matrix $A$ exists. The set of $p \times n$ matrices $A$ for which $g(x) = f(x) + Ax$ is an immersion on $\mathbb{R}^n$ is residual in $\mathbb{R}^{p \times n}$, so the answer is **yes** without any bound on the size of the entries of $A$.

